igquery is very common into  neo4j and then you compute graph  embeddings so the graph embeddings I  believe neo4j offers five different  algorithms to do that these are things  like Fast RP algorithms that came out of  Academia specifically to do this kind of  thing and in that scenario data comes  from some system data lands in neo4j  graph embedding is computed and then the  data is exported elsewhere where it's  made use of by some Downstream system  that isn't even related to neo4j  so we really see these two different  patterns either where we're an active  system of record as part of the data  pipeline or we're contributing uh some  expertise based on the algorithms we  offer  oh that's awesome and then in terms of  data science Bloom this is a kind of  visualization tool right like a bi  visualization tool can you talk about  how that's used yeah so kind of starting  at 30 000 feet you've got the graph  database which it's a database you know  it has a query language you can interact  with that you've got graph data science  which is these implementations of all  these graph algorithms 50 some odd  different algorithms for clustering  centrality embeddings things like that  and then finally what you just mentioned  there's a technology called Bloom and  this sits on top of the database and  it's a business intelligence tool and  while some of our users interact with  traditional business intelligence tools  you know things like looker Tableau  click spot fire stuff like that bloom  can be useful for for particular use  cases where you really want to take  advantage of the graph data and that's  because Bloom is really the only  business intelligence tool I know of  that was built from the ground up  focused squarely on graphs so it lets  you explore the graphs interactively  color nodes and edges different ways to  better visualize things and then one of  the newest features I was just added  probably a month ago or so you can  actually make calls in bloom i